<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fergus Mór</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 500</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Fergus Mór mac Eirc</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fergus Mór mac Eirc was a legendary king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> who, according to later tradition, moved the dynasty's seat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">County Antrim</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a> around 500. The kings of Dál Riata and the later kings of Scots traced their descent from him. His historical existence is uncertain, as the stories were shaped by medieval genealogists.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fergus Mór, Fergus the Great, was said to be the son of Erc of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dalriada</a>. The king lists of Dál Riata and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, such as the Senchus Fer n-Alban and the Duan Albanach, name his father as Erc son of Eochaid Muinremuir, and the Senchus gives Fergus the further name Mac Nisse Mór. A Middle Irish genealogy of the kings of Alba carries his line back through dozens of generations. In Patrician tradition and symbolic accounts the sons of Erc are given as twelve.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The only record of his career is an entry in the Annals of Tigernach saying that Fergus Mór mac Eirc held part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> with the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> and died there. The forms of the names in it were written down long after the 6th century, and the theories of an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a> from Ireland built on it are not considered authentic. A reference to the Fergus tradition in the Irish notulae of the Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a>, copied in the 9th century in older language, shows that he was not simply a product of 10th-century propaganda.</p><p>Only one king in 6th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, Ceretic of Alt Clut, is known from contemporary evidence, and the first kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> whose existence is reasonably sure are Fergus's grandsons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabran-mac-domangairt">Gabrán mac Domangairt</a> and Comgall, or his great-grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedan-mac-gabrain">Áedán mac Gabráin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Later writers made Fergus the founder of the Scottish kingdom. Andrew of Wyntoun's Orygynale Cronykil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, of the early 15th century, called him the first Scot to rule in Scotland and said he brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-of-scone">Stone of Scone</a> from Ireland. George Buchanan, following John of Fordun, told of a Fergus raised in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> who reconquered the Scottish lands. James VI called himself the monarch sprung of Fergus's race, and the Great Gallery of the Palace of Holyroodhouse holds Jacob de Wet's portraits of Scottish monarchs from Fergus to Charles II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fergus Mór” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Irish annals and later Scottish tradition record that <b>Fergus Mór mac Eirc</b> ‘took part of Britain’ with the people of Dál Riata and died there, an event entered under about 501, and medieval writers presented the kingdom as the product of a migration from Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Cenel Loairn claimed descent from Loarn mac Eirc, said to be a brother of <b>Fergus Mor</b>, the legendary founder of Dal Riata.</blockquote><a href="/state/cenel-loairn">Cenel Loairn</a></li><li><blockquote>Cenel nGabrain traced its descent to Gabran, said to be a grandson of <b>Fergus Mor</b>, and held Kintyre, Arran and nearby lands in the 6th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/cenel-ngabrain">Cenel nGabrain</a></li></ul></section>
